<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Robots on Earth and in Space</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Future Exploration of Space</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Summarize discussions from academic texts. Reading: Extract information from reports on researched topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can summarize academic texts on robotics, identifying key concepts (e.g., "The text explains that robots are used to automate repetitive tasks, improving efficiency in industries like manufacturing and healthcare.")<br>Can extract information from reports on technology in Panama (e.g., "The report shows that robots are increasingly being used in Panama's healthcare sector to assist with surgery and patient care.")<br>Can discuss findings from reports on technology and space (e.g., "The report explains how NASA's Mars rover collects samples autonomously and compares it to local Panamanian projects using drones to monitor biodiversity.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key concepts and pronunciation related to "Robots on Earth and in Space" to prepare students for reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a captivating image of a future space station with robots operating, or a robot assisting in a factory. Students are given 30 seconds to silently observe the image. Then, they quickly read a list of 5 key vocabulary words from the previous lesson (e.g., <em>automation, exploration, mission, efficient, operate</em>) and silently match them to elements they see in the picture. Students then share one word and their reason for matching it. This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher projects a short paragraph (2-3 sentences) on the board containing key vocabulary and grammar for the lesson (e.g., "By 2050, robots will have explored distant planets, and data will be collected autonomously. If a machine operates continuously, it needs regular repair."). The teacher reads the paragraph aloud, modeling the pronunciation of /r/ in "robots," /s/ in "space," and -ed endings in "explored" (/d/) and "collected" (/ɪd/). The teacher highlights these words. Students echo-read individually and in chorus.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main topic of our warm-up picture and paragraph? Is it about the past, present, or future of technology?" (Expected answer: Future of technology/space exploration.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'explored' or 'collected,' do the robots do the action or is the action done to them? What kind of voice is that?" (Expected answer: Action is done by/to them, passive voice.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Robots on Earth and in Space," focusing on reading comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic academic text excerpt (250-300 words) titled "Robotics in Space Exploration: Past, Present, and Future Missions." The text uses formal register and is structured with an introduction and two body paragraphs. It covers topics like Mars rovers, satellite repair, and future autonomous exploration. The text integrates vocabulary such as <em>robot, technology, space, mission, orbit, collect, explore, automated, efficient, repair</em>, and grammar such as future perfect ("By 2030, new rockets will have launched many more satellites."), passive voice ("Data is collected by rovers."), and zero conditional ("If a robot senses an obstacle, it stops.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension for specific information and gist from academic texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text quickly (2 minutes) to identify the main purpose. Teacher asks: "What is the general topic of this article? Is it mostly about robots helping people on Earth or exploring space?" (Multiple choice or open discussion).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the text more carefully to determine if the following statements are true or false.</li>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"By the next decade, humans will have fully replaced robots in dangerous space missions." (False)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Data collected by space robots is essential for scientific research." (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a satellite needs repair, astronauts must always perform the task manually." (False)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key vocabulary from the text (e.g., <em>automation, telescope, gravity</em>) with their definitions or examples provided in another column.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading: identifying and understanding target grammar and vocabulary within the text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific grammatical structures (future perfect, passive voice, zero conditional) and integrating key vocabulary (<em>robot, technology, automation, space, mission, operate, collect</em>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Reading & Grammar Focus - Individual):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences extracted from the main text, but with blanks where the target grammar structures or vocabulary words should be. They must fill in the blanks, choosing from a word bank or conjugating verbs appropriately. For example: "By 2040, humans ______ (not discover) all secrets of the universe, but robots _______ (collect) vast amounts of data." or "Important ______ (information) is ______ (transmit) from the Mars rover daily."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Reading & Summarizing - Pairs):</b> In pairs, students re-read specific sections of the main text. Each pair is assigned one paragraph. They must identify the main idea and two supporting details, then write a 2-3 sentence summary of their assigned paragraph, ensuring they use at least two vocabulary words and one grammar structure (future perfect, passive, or zero conditional) from the lesson in their summary. For example, "The text explains that if robots are programmed efficiently, they will have completed complex missions by the next decade."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Peer Check & Discussion - Pairs):</b> Pairs exchange their paragraph summaries and read them. They check if the summary accurately reflects the paragraph's content, if the target vocabulary is used correctly, and if the grammar is intelligible. They then briefly discuss: "Does your partner's summary clearly explain the paragraph? Why might some people worry about the rise of robotics in space exploration mentioned in the text?" (Pragmatic function).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Robots on Earth and in Space," focusing on summarization and information extraction from different reports.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Mini-Report Comparison.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives three different, short (100-150 words) "reports" or "articles" on robotics.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Report A: "Robots in Panama's Healthcare Sector: Current Applications." (Focuses on medicine, automation, efficiency, passive voice: "Surgeries are assisted by robotic arms.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Report B: "Agricultural Automation: The Future of Farming in Panama." (Focuses on agriculture, mechanical equipment, program, future perfect: "By 2030, many crops will have been harvested by automated machines.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Report C: "Panama's Role in Space Technology: Satellite Monitoring and Research." (Focuses on space, satellite, research, collect, zero conditional: "If a satellite detects irregular weather patterns, data is immediately transmitted.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group member chooses one report to read individually. Their task is to extract the key information, summarizing the main findings of their chosen report (Learning Outcome: "Can extract information from reports on technology in Panama").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After individual reading and summarizing (5-7 minutes), group members share their summaries orally with their group. They compare the different applications of robotics (on Earth and in space), highlighting similarities and differences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The group then collaboratively discusses their findings, encouraging respectful dialogue on diverse perspectives (Pragmatic function) and offering solutions or future predictions (Sociolinguistic function). For example, "The report explains how NASA's Mars rover collects samples autonomously; we could compare that to local Panamanian projects using drones to monitor biodiversity."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">As a group, they prepare a brief, shared summary (1-2 sentences) that synthesizes the key impacts of robotics discussed in their three reports, using future perfect or passive voice (Learning Outcome: "Can summarize academic texts on robotics, identifying key concepts").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' reading comprehension, summarization, and information extraction skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and a short exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups present their synthesized 1-2 sentence summary to the class (optional, or teacher collects the written summary). The teacher listens for key information extracted and proper use of target language. Additionally, students complete an exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to extract key information from assigned report.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy of individual summary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., <em>automation, industry, medicine, space</em>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of passive voice or future perfect.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active participation in group discussion and offering viewpoints.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting piece of information you extracted from your report today regarding robotics on Earth or in space?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new vocabulary word I learned to use effectively in a sentence is __________ because ___________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson, and connection to the next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did you face when reading the academic texts, and how did you overcome them?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did summarizing and discussing the reports with your group help you understand the topic better?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> What worked well in this reading lesson? Were students able to summarize and extract information effectively? Was the text level appropriate for B1.3? Did the grammar points (future perfect, passive voice, zero conditional) come through clearly in the reading tasks? What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled with academic text comprehension?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will use the information extracted and summarized today to participate in a structured debate or discussion about the ethical implications and future potential of robotics on Earth and in space, actively employing the target vocabulary and grammar in oral communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>